    "question": {
      ":question/id": "a3f18187-37ce-5dc7-8cc4-e068fb1e6a24",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=t^{2} - 2,\\,y=6 t^{5} + t^{3},\\,0\\le t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 t,\\,y'(t)=3 t^{2} \\left(10 t^{2} + 1\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(3 t^{2} \\left(10 t^{2} + 1\\right)\\right)}{\\left(2 t\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3 t \\left(10 t^{2} + 1\\right)}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right) \\left(10 \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 1\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{33}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
