      ":question/problem": "For the parametric curve \n\n$$\nx=6 t^{2} - 1,\\,y=t^{5} + t^{3},\\,0\\le t<\\infty,\n$$\n\ncalculate the slope of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We will use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{dy}{dx}=\\frac{y'(t)}{x'(t)}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $x$ and $y,$ we get\n\n$$\nx'(t)=12 t,\\,y'(t)=t^{2} \\left(5 t^{2} + 3\\right).\n$$\n\n \n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(t^{2} \\left(5 t^{2} + 3\\right)\\right)}{\\left(12 t\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{t \\left(5 t^{2} + 3\\right)}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, when $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent to the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{\\left(2\\right) \\left(5 \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 3\\right)}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{23}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
